        # ReleaseNotes of this module
        ReleaseNotes = 'v0.14.0: Remote sessions. New: ps-bash session attach NAME gives a reattachable terminal (tmux-style) - a detached broker owns a PTY and the interactive shell, so the shell and its screen outlive the client; Ctrl-] d detaches, a new client gets the last 1 MiB of output replayed, several clients can attach (--read-only to watch), and session stop ends it. Remote use is ssh -t host ps-bash session attach NAME. Works on Linux, macOS and Windows (ConPTY); the wire protocol is documented in docs/sessions.md. Named sessions (PSBASH_SESSION=NAME) are now stateful by default: variables, cd and functions persist between commands in one pinned runspace (--stateless restores a fresh runspace per command); session list shows each mode. Changed: the Windows named-pipe transport is removed; AF_UNIX sockets are the only transport (a socket path over the OS limit now fails with a message naming TMPDIR). Fix: a shell started under a Windows pseudoconsole no longer inherits the parent redirected stdio (it read EOF and exited with no prompt), so PSBASH_PTY=1 works when ps-bash is launched from a pipe-driven parent. v0.13.0: Release integrity and safety. SECURITY: tar extraction refuses to write through an existing symlink or junction below the destination (it could escape the directory and still report success). New: named durable sessions - ps-bash session start|list|status|stop NAME plus PSBASH_SESSION=NAME give a host that outlives its launchers and runs concurrently with other sessions (docs/sessions.md). New: history lists entries, history -d ID deletes one, history -c erases all (secure delete, WAL truncate, VACUUM); a line starting with a space, or any line while PSBASH_HISTORY_PRIVATE=1, is never recorded; an unopenable history DB degrades to in-memory with a warning. Fix: install.sh and install.ps1 install the complete package (host and runtime files), smoke-test a real command before switching, keep the previous version for rollback, and name an unsupported platform. Release pipeline: binaries are built from one pinned commit, smoke-tested, and published only after the tests pass; dry-run mode rehearses a branch. Test harness: deadlines are checkpoints that extend while the machine is starved. v0.12.0: Native commands now get their piped stdin as raw bytes (no more added \r\n per record, binary input intact) through the new ConvertTo-BashNativeStdin step the transpiler emits ahead of a native command; cat feeds natives the same way. Fix: mv/cp replacing an in-use destination never deletes its own source when the source is named like a leftover (.name.psbash-replaced-*), and a copy that fails part-way restores the original destination instead of losing it. Fix: a pattern with thousands of nested extglob groups ([[ x == @(@(@(...)))]]) is a clean error, not a host-killing stack overflow. Fix: source of a file named Invoke-BashSource.sh works; sourcing an empty file sets $? to 0 as bash does. v0.11.6: Fix (#7): ps-bash -c no longer piles up idle ps-bash-host processes under an AI agent. The warm background host now exits about 5 seconds after the shell or agent session that started it ends, instead of idling for 10 minutes; an agent that ran each command in a fresh shell used to leave one ~65 MB host per command. Warm reuse inside a live session is unchanged. New docs page The Host Daemon explains sessions, when the host exits, and PSBASH_SESSION / PSBASH_PER_INVOCATION / PSBASH_HOST_IDLE_SECS. Fix: a command that stops reading piped stdin early (yes | ps-bash -c ''head -n 1'') no longer exits 125 with "host dropped the connection" on Linux/macOS. Fix: cd ~/dir works on Linux/macOS (the path after ~ was joined with a backslash). Fix: $HOME and ~ follow the calling shell''s HOME when commands share a background host. Fix: release zips report the right version in ps-bash --version (a v0.11.5 zip said 0.10.19). New: on WSL, the first unknown command warns when Windows /mnt directories on PATH make command lookups slow, and says how to turn them off. v0.11.5: Fixes: mv and cp on Windows replace a destination that is read-only or in use - including a running program (mv new.exe app.exe) - as Linux does, instead of "Access to the path is denied"; a file another process truly holds open is reported at once, naming the process ("Device or resource busy (open in app.exe, pid N)"), with nothing changed; a directory moves across drives. A sourced file''s output now streams in order. [[ $a == $b ]] treats an unquoted $b as a pattern, and [[ $PWD == /tmp* ]] matches after cd /tmp. v0.11.4: Fixes: unquoted $f.txt, $f:x and ${f}x, and quoted "${f}x" / "${f}_bak", now expand f and keep the suffix (they printed empty or named another variable); break N / continue N leave the right loop, and a break inside while read ... done < file no longer exits the whole script; case, [[ == ]] and [ = ] match case-sensitively like bash (shopt -s nocasematch supported) and case arms are tried in order; a sourced file''s output reaches stdout, and source keeps the caller''s $1.. (restoring them after source f args). v0.11.3: SAFETY: rm -rf "$unset" (an empty operand) no longer deletes the current directory''s contents (data loss in 0.11.2); every command treats an empty path as missing. The host now survives hostile input (deep nesting, huge chains, brace bombs, catastrophic regexes, runaway recursion and memory, kill -9 $$): only that command fails, and every host exit is logged with a reason. Fixes: heredocs and parens inside "$(...)" (git commit -m "$(cat <<''EOF'' ...)" with it''s or :) failed to parse); set -e stops on any failing command; if/while conditions keep their output; || and && see return N; subshells no longer leak set -e, variables or shopt; >&2 obeys 2>/dev/null and 2>&1; 2> files are byte-exact; repeated redirects; a failed redirect never runs its command; grep/sed BRE \| \( \{ \+ \?; expr :; extglob in case and [[ ]]; literal and expanded /tmp paths agree; date +%N; concurrent commands in the codex sandbox; locked files report Permission denied. v0.11.2: fixes a v0.11.0 regression where every native command (git, wsl, tman...) hung under an agent shell tool whose stdin is open but never written: natives now get the shell stdin as a real OS pipe, and what they do not read stays for the next command. Also: cmd < /dev/null means empty input; cd/eval/source/read/set keep their redirects; declare/typeset -f/-F/-p implemented (no more type: -f: not found); command -v, type and which print bash names instead of Invoke-Bash* and follow bash exit codes; BASH_VERSION and --version report the real version instead of 0.8.0. v0.11.1: maintenance — zero compiler warnings in PsBash.Core.Tests (nullability, xUnit analyzers, platform attributes, a duplicate test case removed) and a much smaller agent instruction context. v0.11.0: GNU-parity release. One shared ordered argument parser now drives ~50 commands (bundles, --, unique-prefix long options, GNU usage errors; a real option ps-bash does not implement is refused with exit 2, never silently ignored). Shell semantics: pathname expansion is a shell step (echo *, for f in *, arrays), $(...) word-splits, : > f creates the file, env prefixes reach pipe stages, compound commands share stdin, the launcher forwards its stdin into -c. Binary-safe bytes end to end (cat of a binary file is byte-exact; printf ''\351'' | wc -c is 1). Commands: find (-printf, -perm, -user, -newerXY, -L ...), grep/rg/sed option sets, ls layouts and sorting, cut -c in bytes, split -n, sort -R/-z/-V suffixes, join -o/-e, uniq --group, cat -A, head/tail -v/-z/-F, wc --total, cp/mv -i/-t/-T/-b/--update, ln -r, tar member filters, GNU diff options, a new jq engine with --arg/-n/-e, awk getline/print redirection/functions. Streaming and memory: grep/sed/find/tail/awk/seq/redirect stream with bounded memory; the host frees output as it is sent. Host: warm nested daemons per depth, cached command-not-found index (missing command 9s -> 1s). Linux/macOS fixes. Intentional differences: docs/specs/intentional-differences.md. v0.10.23: Daemon stall fix, release-archive fix, and a transpiler correctness batch from a real-world .sh corpus sweep. Host: a command whose launcher died mid-flight kept running in the daemon while holding the process-wide execution gate, so every later command in that session queued behind work nobody would ever read — a launcher killed 2s into sleep 30 made the next command wait 30.9s; now 0.2s. Connections now detect client disconnect (EOF, on live duplex transports only) and cancel the abandoned command, and WorkerPool.DisposeAsync awaits in-flight warmers so no runspace outlives its pool. Packaging: every release archive since v0.9.1 shipped without System.Management.Automation.dll and failed every command with "ps-bash-host did not accept connections within 20s" — PrivateAssets=all on the Microsoft.PowerShell.SDK reference excluded the SDK runtime assets from publish. Fixed, and build.yml plus publish.yml now RUN the packaged archive (scripts/smoke-archive.ps1) before uploading, which no workflow previously did. Transpiler, every fix oracle-verified against bash: command substitution as an arithmetic operand, $(( $(date +%s) + 60 )); empty case-arm bodies no longer swallow their own terminator; [[ x =~ ^(a|b)$ ]] keeps its regex; array literals spanning newlines, and declare/readonly/local initializers, survive; here-strings on compound commands; single-item for-in; while read a b binds every name; [[ ( … ) ]] grouping; quoted test operands keep expansions live; compound commands as &&/|| operands; braced ${name} before a colon; nested quotes inside "$( … )"; $(<file). Runtime: POSIX bracket classes such as [[:digit:]] and [[:space:]] never worked in grep/sed/awk/rg and silently matched nothing — they are now translated for .NET; binder-swallowed-flag recovery no longer reads a different pipeline stage.'

    } # End of PSData hashtable

